doc.para:
Classes
　A class is a blueprint used when creating objects. Variables belonging to a class are called member variables, functions belonging to a class are called member functions or methods, and properties belonging to a class are called member properties.

　Basically, a class is declared as follows:

class classname
{
	// Write members, member methods, and member properties here

	function classname() // Constructor
	{
	}

	function finalize()
	{
	}
}



　Specify an identifier for the class name.

　Inside the class, write the variables, methods, and properties that will become members when that class is created.

　Inside a class, there is always one method with the exact same name as the class, which is called the constructor. When an object is created using the new operator, this function is called along with the arguments passed to the new operator.

　Similarly, inside a class, there is a special method called finalize. This is a method called when an object is destroyed, and it can be omitted. You do not have to write it.


	class test
	{
		function test()
		{
			// Constructor
			// Write the processing to be executed when the object is created here
			variable = 0;
		}

		function finalize()
		{
			// finalize method
			// Write the processing to be executed when the object is destroyed here
		}

		function method1()
		{
			// Method
			System.inform(variable);
		}

		var variable; // Member variable

		property prop // Member property
		{
			getter() { return variable; } 
		}
	}


When using the instanceof operator with "Class" on a class, it evaluates to true (in the above example, test instanceof "Class" is true).

Creating Objects
　To create an object of a declared class, use the new operator.
　After the new operator, specify the class name and the arguments to pass to the constructor, just like a function call.

	class Test
	{
		var variable1 = getValue(); // Initialization of member variable

		function Test(arg1, arg2) // Constructor
		{
			// Here, the arguments specified in the new operator are passed to arg1 and arg2
		}
	}

	var newobject = new Test(1, 2); // Create an object of test class by passing 1, 2 as arguments


　The order of processing when a class object is created is as follows:



First, an empty object is created

Methods and properties are registered

Member variables are created (at this time, variables that require initialization are initialized)

The constructor is executed



　Even if there are no arguments to the constructor, the ( ) after the new operator cannot be omitted. Unlike JavaScript, you cannot write new Test. You must write new Test( ).



　In particular, when creating an object of that class within a method or property of the class, or when creating an object of that class's superclass, writing it as follows will result in an error:

	class Test
	{
		function Test() // Constructor
		{
		}

		function func()
		{
			return new Test(); // Error
		}
	}


　This is because simply writing Test inside a method or property of a class refers to the constructor Test rather than the class Test due to scope proximity. To avoid this, you need to explicitly use global. as shown below (since classes are registered in global).


	class Test
	{
		function Test() // Constructor
		{
		}

		function func()
		{
			return new global.Test(); // This is OK
		}
	}

Object Invalidation
　In TJS2, when an object is deleted, it goes through two stages: invalidation of the object and deletion of the object.
　When an object is invalidated, the finalize method is called, and the object is marked as invalid. Subsequent access to that object will fail and cause an exception. You can check whether an object is invalidated using the isvalid operator.

　Objects can be invalidated using the invalidate operator.


	class Test
	{
		var variable;

		function Test()
		{
			// Constructor
			variable = new AnotherClass();
		}

		function finalize()
		{
			// The finalize method is called when invalidated
			invalidate variable;
		}
	}

	var object = new Test(); // Create an object

	(omitted)

	invalidate object; // Invalidate the object


　Even without using the invalidate operator, an object will be deleted when it is no longer needed. If it is not invalidated at that point, it will be invalidated then.
　In TJS2, there is no explicit rule as to when an object is deleted, meaning deletion and invalidation "can happen at any time". Therefore, since an object that has not been invalidated will be invalidated upon deletion, the finalize method might be called at an unexpected moment. To prevent this, it is recommended to invalidate objects using the invalidate operator once you are done using them.


　The invalidate operator works similarly to the C++ delete operator.
　Unlike C++'s delete operator, TJS2's delete operator is used to delete members or local variables. It does not invalidate or delete the object itself, but deleting a member or local variable may result in the object being invalidated or deleted.

Inheritance
　Using the keyword extends, you can make a class inherit from another class. Inheritance means taking over the members of the inherited base class.
　The class being inherited from is called a superclass, and the inheriting class is called a subclass.
　Declare classes as follows:

	class Class1 // Superclass
	{
		function Class1() // Class1 constructor
		{
		}

		function finalize() // Class1 finalize
		{
		}

		function method1() // method1
		{
		}
	}


	class Class2 extends Class1
	{
		function Class2() // Class2 constructor
		{
			super.Class1(); // Call Class1 constructor
		}

		function finalize() // Class2 finalize
		{
			super.finalize();
		}
	}

	var obj = new Class2(); // Create Class2 object
	obj.method1(); // Class2 inherits Class1's method1, so it can be used

　In the above example, Class2 inherits from Class1. We can also say Class2 is derived from Class1.
　Inside Class2's constructor, Class1's constructor is called, and inside Class2's finalize, Class1's finalize is called. Behavior when a subclass does not call the corresponding method of the superclass within these methods is undefined (or rather, the current version does not check whether it was called or not), so please be sure to write it.

　To refer to the superclass from a subclass, you can use the keyword super as in the example above. This keyword can only be used inside a subclass and represents the superclass.

　When inheritance is involved, the order of object initialization with the new operator is as follows:



First, an empty object is created

Methods and properties are registered (starting from the superclass towards the subclass)

Member variables are created (starting from the superclass towards the subclass)

The subclass constructor is called

(From within the subclass constructor) The superclass constructor is called

Multiple Inheritance
　By listing multiple superclass names after extends, you can perform multiple inheritance.

	class SubClass extends ClassA, ClassB
	{
		function SubClass() // SubClass constructor
		{
			ClassA(); // Call ClassA constructor
			ClassB(); // Call ClassB constructor
		}

		function finalize() // Class2 finalize
		{
			global.ClassA.finalize();
			global.ClassB.finalize();
		}
	}


　Since the super keyword cannot be used, you must explicitly specify the class. The reason why global. is explicitly attached to access the class when calling the superclass method in the example is because writing simply ClassA inside a subclass refers to ClassA's constructor. Since the class itself is registered in global, global. is used to access the class.

　When multiple inheritance is used, the order of object initialization with the new operator is the same as when not using multiple inheritance, but the order of registering superclass methods and properties is done in the order they are written after the extends keyword. If there are methods or properties with the same name among the base classes, the one written later takes precedence. Hidden methods or properties can be accessed by explicitly writing the class name, such as global.ClassA.hiddenMethod().

Overriding
　Declaring a method or member property in a subclass with the same name as one in the superclass allows you to hide the superclass method. This is called overriding.
　The finalize method mentioned in the explanation above overrides the superclass's finalize method.

	class Class1 // Superclass
	{
		function Class1() // Class1 constructor
		{
		}

		function finalize() // Class1 finalize
		{
		}

		function method1() // method1
		{
			(omitted)
		}
	}


	class Class2 extends Class1
	{
		function Class2() // Class2 constructor
		{
			super.Class1(); // Call Class1 constructor
		}

		function finalize() // Class2 finalize
		{
			super.finalize();
		}

		function method1() // Override Class1.method1
		{
			(omitted)
			if(omitted) return super.method1();
			(omitted)
		}
	}

	var obj = new Class2(); // Create Class2 object
	obj.method1(); // Class2's method1 is called

　In methods or properties of a subclass, you can access methods or properties of the superclass using the super keyword.

　Member variables cannot be overridden. Since member variables are registered to a single object, if there are member variables of the same name in the subclass and superclass, the subclass's variable will overwrite the superclass's variable.
